This scenario simulates a phishing attack as it unfolds in real time. The objectives are:

Monitor and analyse real-time alerts as the attack unfolds.
Identify and document critical events such as PowerShell executions, reverse shell connections, and suspicious DNS requests.
Create detailed case reports based on your observations to help the team understand the full scope of the breach.



ALERT 1 - SUSPICIOUS EMAIL FROM EXTERNAL DOMAIN
Sender: eileen@trendymillineryco.me
The body of the email uses tactics of urgency and promise of monetary gain to gather sensitive information (banking details). These are very commonly used tactics in phishing attempts. The unusual sender domain combined with the phishing tactics make this suspicious, and this is a true positive. The SOC lead sent the alert to us stating that the rule needs fine tuning.
I classified this as a true positive in my report. The recommended action is to blocking the sender's domain at the email filter. If there were any links or attachments to open, recommendations would also include additional investigation of the account and the host to see if any related activity (suspicious processes, suspicious network activity, suspicious logins, etc) has occurred. 

ALERT 2 - SUSPICIOUS PARENT-CHILD PROCESS RELATIONSHIP
TrustedInstaller.exe is executing with a parent of services.exe. The process's working directory is C:\Windows\system32\, which is suspicious when combined with the fact that "Trusted" is in the process name. This process was run at 05/30/2026 20:04:51.219 on the win-3459 machine, which belongs to Michelle in Legal. The legal department is also a commonly targeted department as they usually have elevated privileges and access to sensitive information.
This was classified as a true positive due to it running from a directory that doesn't normally run 3rd party tools. This indicates suspicious activity, especially when the executable also claims to be Trusted in the name. This is meant to deter the average user from investigating further. The recommendation is to block/kill the process.

TRIAGE AND ALERT 3 - SUSPICIOUS PARENT-CHILD PROCESS RELATIONSHIP
After closing the previous alert, there were 3 more incoming alerts. They consisted of another suspicious parent-child relationship and 2 more suspicious emails. The suspicious process alert has a higher likelihood of affecting the organization, so it gets triaged first.
This relationship is taskhostw.exe running with a parent of svchost.exe. It also had a CLI command of: taskhostw.exe KEYROAMING. The timestamp of this activity was at 05/30/2026 20:07:15.219 and was executed on win-3451 (which belongs to Miguel in Sales).
After investigation, this is likely a benign process running a background service.

ALERT 4 - SUSPICIOUS ATTACHMENT IN EMAIL
There were still 2 other suspicious emails waiting, but this one was triaged above those as a suspicious attachment is more likely to involve interaction. Subsequently, it also is likely to have a more disastrous effect on the organization if interacted with.
The subject is: FINAL NOTICE: Overdue Payment - Account Suspension Imminent
This is already raising red flags because this is URGENTly asking for MONEY with THREAT of account suspension.
john@hatmakereurope.xyz is the sender. .xyz domains can be used legitimately, but also maliciously. This entirely depends on context.
It targeted michael.ascot@tryhatme.com who is the CEO, the PRIME high profile target.
And the attachment is a zip: ImportantInvoice-Febrary.zip. An invoice would typically be a .doc or .pdf. A zip folder indicates there are going to be other files in the folder, which is potentially malicious.
The timestamp on this email was 05/30/2026 20:12:35.219, which may be needed later for correlation and timeline development.
The recommendation is to block the sender at the email filter and investigate the CEO's workstation for evidence of interaction with the attachment.

ALERT 5 - SUSPICIOUS PARENT-CHILD PROCESS RELATIONSHIP
my mind immediately went to the need to check if these were run on the CEO's computer and when.
The first process is rdpclip.exe with a parent of svchost.exe (not the normal parent) and a timestamp of 05/30/2026 20:13:47.219. Merely one minute after the suspicious email was received, the CEO's workstation is seen running this process. In itself, the process is only moderately suspicious, based on the parent process. However, combined with the fact that it came almost immediately after a likely click on a potentially malicious attachment, makes this highly suspect.
This process needs to be killed and the host analyzed for further IOCs. Another recommendation is to isolate the host, as potentially malicious activity is now being seen.

