ALERT 10 - SUSPICIOUS EMAIL FROM EXTERNAL DOMAIN
I performed a quick triage analysis of these, as several suspicious parent-child process relationships came up while investigating the first email. After a short analysis, it is determined that, while these are likely suspicious (containing phishing tactics such as urgency and promise of money), they do not contain links or attachments. I left these in the queue for further investigation of the sender domain later and moved onto the High priority process alerts.

ALERTS 11-20 - SUSPICIOUS PARENT-CHILD PROCESS RELATIONSHIP
There were 10 of these High priority alerts in total. They were all the same process (nslookup) on the same machine (CEO's workstation) with slightly altered command lines. 
10 commands were run between 05/30/2026 20:37:45.219 -  05/30/2026 20:38:01.219, which is about 16 seconds total. Multiple commands being run within extremely quick succession suggests potential automation, which would likely have been prompted by a remote malicious actor. Additionally, all of the lookups were to encoded URLs at domains like haz4rdw4re.io. For example: UEsDBBQAAAAIANigLlfVU3cDIgAAAI.haz4rdw4re.io. This looks highly suspect. That encoded portion being queried actually decrypts to a PZIP archive itself, as well. This all signals potential command and control (C2). The host should be isolated immediately to prevent further spread of this infection. 

ALERTS 21-24 - NETWORK DRIVE DISCONNECTED, SUSPICIOUS PARENT-CHILD PROCESS RELATIONSHIP, NETWORK DRIVE MAPPED TO LOCAL DRIVE, and POWERSHELL SCRIPT IN DOWNLOADS FOLDER
These were also all on the CEO's workstation, which prompted immediate triage. If we read them backwards (in the order they were alerted rather than the order listed in the SIEM), a PowerShell script was downloaded (likely from one of the domains looked up previously), a network drive (these usually contain important information) was mapped to the CEO's computer, a suspicious process was run (robocopy.exe run from PowerShell), and then the drive was disconnected.
The network drive mapped and then disconnected was: "C:\Windows\system32\net.exe" use Z: \\FILESRV-01\SSF-FinancialRecords
Additionally, this command was run as part of the suspicious process relationship: "C:\Windows\system32\Robocopy.exe" . C:\Users\michael.ascot\downloads\exfiltration /E
All 4 of these alerts came in within under 3 minutes, from first to last.
A timeline is starting to come together, from phishing email to C2 traffic, to exfiltration.
Data exfiltration confirmed based on the network drive accessed and the robocopy and exfiltration command run. The recommendation is to isolate the host IMMEDIATELY to prevent further damage and start the incident response plans.

FINAL RESULT AND RECOMMENDATIONS
The CEO of the company received a suspicious (phishing) email with a ZIP folder attached. Suspicious activity was then seen on his workstation, such as 10 nslookup commands to encoded URLs within the span of 16 seconds. This indicated potential command and control (C2) activity. Then, it is alerted and confirmed that a PowerShell script was downloaded, a network drive containing financial records was mapped to his workstation, data exfiltration occurred, and finally, the network drive was disconnected. All of these events happened very quickly. It was recommended to isolate his workstation from the network to prevent further spread and immediately activate incident response plans. 

